This was based on the 3.15 PR: https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/pull/43129/files

Fixes issue dotnet/runtime#57472

Main pr dotnet/runtime#57479

Also backported to 5.0 in dotnet/runtime#57562 and 3.1 in #43102. This PR will pave the way for backporting it to the `System.Memory` OOB package for .NET Framework.

# Customer Impact

A customer has encountered dotnet/runtime#57472 while on .NET Framework and reported it in dotnet/runtime#67295, necessiating backporting it once again. As an example provided in that issue, this memory manager:

``` csharp
private sealed class BasicMemoryManager : MemoryManager<byte>
{
    private readonly byte[] _buffer;
    public BasicMemoryManager(int size = 1024) => _buffer = new byte[size];

    protected override bool TryGetArray(out ArraySegment<byte> segment)
    {
        segment = new ArraySegment<byte>(_buffer);
        return true;
    }
    public override Span<byte> GetSpan() => _buffer;

    public override MemoryHandle Pin(int elementIndex = 0) => throw new NotSupportedException();
    public override void Unpin() => throw new NotSupportedException();
    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing) { }
}
```

will fail two of the following tests:

``` csharp
[Theory]
[InlineData(0, 32)]
[InlineData(0, 29)]
[InlineData(32, 32)] // fail: length 0
[InlineData(32, 29)] // fail: length -3
public void OffsetTest(int offset, int length)
{
    var mgr = new BasicMemoryManager(1024);
    var slice = mgr.Memory.Slice(offset, length);
    Assert.Equal(length, slice.Length);

    var ros = new ReadOnlySequence<byte>(slice);
    Assert.True(ros.IsSingleSegment);
    Assert.Equal(length, ros.Length);
}
```

# Testing

Failing test has been added in this PR.

# Risk

Low, as the change is very simple and obvious when you look at `ReadOnlySequence<T>` ctor that accepts `ReadOnlyMemory<T>` (notice line 133 versus lines 140 and 148):

https://github.com/dotnet/corefx/blob/0d40b482e6b6c232131dc808182d6b32d9d37394/src/System.Memory/src/System/Buffers/ReadOnlySequence.cs#L130-L149

Moreover, the changed code gets triggered only when creating a `ReadOnlySequence` from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by a `MemoryManager`. All other use cases (including creating from an array or from a `ReadOnlyMemory` backed by an array or a string) are unaffected. And the changes are identical to those in the other backports.

# Regression

This is not a regression.

**Version Used:** .NET SDK 11.0.100-preview.7.26381.103, compiler 5.10.0-1.26381.103 (e2c1e00). Also reproduces identically on 11.0.100-preview.5.26302.115, compiler 5.8.0-1.26302.115 (f7b4c57). `net11.0`, `<LangVersion>preview</LangVersion>`, `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`.

I was exploring how the new unions map onto protobuf `oneof` and ended up poking at nullable value types as case types. Three related things fall out; they all trace back to resolutions in the speclet that don't appear to be implemented yet, so this may just be "not done yet" — but the second one is a silent wrong answer, so it seemed worth writing down.

The speclet I'm reading is [proposals/csharp-15.0/unions.md](https://github.com/dotnet/csharplang/blob/main/proposals/csharp-15.0/unions.md).

## Repro

```csharp
using System;
using System.Linq;
using System.Runtime.CompilerServices;

// (1) Both case types are `int` under the approved resolution, yet this compiles.
public union Both(int?, int);

// (3) Value is annotated `object?` on all three, but only the latter two warn.
public union NoNull(int, string);
public union NullVT(int?, string);
public union NullRef(int, string?);

// (2) Hand-authored union whose case type is `int?`, with a `TryGetValue(out int?)`.
[Union]
public struct U
{
    private readonly int _i;
    private readonly bool _hasI;
    private readonly string? _s;
    private readonly byte _tag;

    public U(int? v) { _i = v ?? 0; _hasI = v.HasValue; _s = null; _tag = 1; }
    public U(string v) { _i = 0; _hasI = false; _s = v; _tag = 2; }

    public object? Value => _tag == 1 ? (_hasI ? _i : null) : _s;
    public bool HasValue => _tag != 0;
    public bool TryGetValue(out int? v) { v = _hasI ? _i : default(int?); return _tag == 1; }
    public bool TryGetValue(out string? v) { v = _s; return _tag == 2; }
}

class Program
{
    static string SwNoNull(NoNull n) => n switch { int i => "i", string s => "s" };
    static string SwNullVT(NullVT n) => n switch { int i => "i", string s => "s" };
    static string SwNullRef(NullRef n) => n switch { int i => "i", string s => "s" };

    static void Main()
    {
        // (1)
        Both a = (int?)5, b = 5;
        Console.WriteLine($"(1) Both: a.Value={a.Value} b.Value={b.Value} a is int={a is int} b is int={b is int}");
        Console.WriteLine($"(1) ctor parameter types: {string.Join(", ", typeof(Both).GetConstructors().OrderBy(c => c.MetadataToken).Select(c => c.GetParameters()[0].ParameterType))}");

        // (2)
        U u = (int?)null;
        Console.WriteLine($"(2) U u = (int?)null;  u.Value is null = {u.Value is null}");
        Console.WriteLine($"(2) u is int x  ->  matched={u is int}, x={(u is int x ? x.ToString() : "<no match>")}");
        Console.WriteLine($"(2) u switch    ->  {u switch { int i => $"int {i}", string s => $"string {s}", _ => "no value" }}");

        // (3)
        foreach (var t in new[] { typeof(NoNull), typeof(NullVT), typeof(NullRef) })
            Console.WriteLine($"(3) {t.Name,-8} type-level nullable attrs: {string.Join(" ", t.GetCustomAttributesData().Where(x => x.AttributeType.Name.StartsWith("Nullable")).Select(x => x.AttributeType.Name + "(" + string.Join(",", x.ConstructorArguments.Select(c => c.Value)) + ")"))}");
        Console.WriteLine($"(3) {SwNoNull(1)}{SwNullVT(1)}{SwNullRef(1)}");
    }
}
```

Diagnostics:

```
Program.cs(34,43): warning CS8655: The switch expression does not handle some null inputs (it is not exhaustive). For example, the pattern 'null' is not covered.
Program.cs(35,45): warning CS8655: The switch expression does not handle some null inputs (it is not exhaustive). For example, the pattern 'null' is not covered.
```

Output:

```
(1) Both: a.Value=5 b.Value=5 a is int=True b is int=True
(1) ctor parameter types: System.Nullable`1[System.Int32], System.Int32
(2) U u = (int?)null;  u.Value is null = True
(2) u is int x  ->  matched=True, x=0
(2) u switch    ->  int 0
(3) NoNull   type-level nullable attrs: NullableContextAttribute(2) NullableAttribute(0)
(3) NullVT   type-level nullable attrs: NullableContextAttribute(2) NullableAttribute(0)
(3) NullRef  type-level nullable attrs: NullableContextAttribute(2) NullableAttribute(0)
(3) iii
```

## (1) `union Both(int?, int)` declares two case types that are the same case

This compiles, and the two cases are indistinguishable at runtime: both store a boxed `int`, both satisfy `is int`. Only the constructor signatures differ, so the CS0111 duplicate-member check doesn't fire.

The speclet has a resolved item on exactly this:

> It feels like we could simply say that, if constructor's/factory's parameter type is a nullable value type, then corresponding case type is the underlying type. Then we wouldn't need that extra clause for the `TryGetValue` method, all out parameters are case types.
>
> **Resolution:** The suggestion is approved

Under that resolution both members of `Both` have case type `int`, and this needs a diagnostic. Today the body of the speclet still describes `int?` as a case type in its own right (`// Union factory method making 'int?' a case type`), so the two halves disagree. Either way there is no rule anywhere in the speclet requiring case types to be distinct — "distinct" and "duplicate" don't appear in it — so the only thing keeping duplicates out is constructor signature collision, which `int?` slips past.

## (2) `TryGetValue(out T?)` produces a match that binds a fabricated value

This is the one I'd call a bug rather than an unimplemented resolution. In `U`, the `int?` case is present but empty. `u.Value` is correctly `null`, and yet:

```
u is int x   ->  matched=True, x=0
```

The pattern reports a successful match against `int` and binds `0`, a value that was never put into the union. A switch behaves the same way — it takes the `int` arm rather than the discard arm.

Instrumenting the members shows `Value` is never consulted: `is int` binds to `TryGetValue(out int? v)`, uses only the `bool` return as the match decision, and unwraps the `Nullable<int>` without checking `HasValue`.

I don't think that method should have been selected at all. The resolved rule for accessor selection is:

> **Resolution:** Only implicit identity, or reference, or boxing conversions are considered

`int` to `int?` is an implicit *nullable* conversion, which is none of those three, so `TryGetValue(out int?)` shouldn't be a candidate for the `int` pattern; falling back to `Value` gives the right answer, since `null` fails `is int`. The design intent is stated explicitly elsewhere in the speclet:

> This is motivated by the fact that a `null` value would never be yielded through this method.

which holds for the `out int` form and cannot hold for the `out int?` form. So either the selection rule needs enforcing, or the signature needs rejecting, or the lowering needs a `HasValue` check — but as it stands a conforming-looking union can silently hand a pattern arm a value it never contained.

Note that the current speclet body does permit this signature:

> A `TryGetValue` method for each case type. The method returns `bool` and takes a single out-parameter of a type that is identity-convertible to either the case type or optionally the underlying value type, if the case type is a nullable value type.

`int?` is identity-convertible to the case type `int?`, so by that clause the method is a legitimate accessor. That clause is what the resolution in (1) was meant to delete.

## (3) Nullability of `Value` still keys off the case types, not the annotation

`SwNoNull` doesn't warn; `SwNullVT` and `SwNullRef` do. That matches the rule in the body of the speclet:

> The default null state of a union type's `Value` property is "maybe null" if the default null state of any of the case types is "maybe null". Otherwise, the default null state is "not null".

But that rule was resolved away:

> Proposal: Remove the quoted rule, nullable analysis should use annotations from the `Value` property to infer its default nullability.
>
> **Resolution:** The proposal is approved

All three generated unions carry identical `NullableContextAttribute(2)` — `Value` is `object?` in every case — so under the annotation-driven rule `SwNoNull` should warn too. Which is the practically interesting case: `default(NoNull)` in a switch covering `int` and `string` throws `SwitchExpressionException`, with nothing at compile time to suggest it. The resolved discussion raises precisely this point (`default(U1).Value` is `null` while analysis says not-null).

## Aside

[#81074](https://github.com/dotnet/roslyn/issues/81074) has no test items for nullable value types as case types, or for `TryGetValue` with a nullable out parameter, which lines up with (1) and (2) both landing there.

Compiling and ILVerifying the following C# code that calls a static abstract method via a constrained type parameter causes ILVerify to report 2 errors:
[IL]: Error [CallAbstract]: [tr161758.dll : tr161758.testclass::testit(!!0)][offset 0x00000007] Call not allowed on abstract methods.
[IL]: Error [Constrained]: [tr161758.dll : tr161758.testclass::testit(!!0)][offset 0x00000007] Missing callvirt following constrained prefix.
2 Error(s) Verifying tr161758.dll

Looking at generated IL it generates the following, so it appears ILVerify is correct
  IL_0001:  constrained. !!T
  IL_0007:  call       void tr161758.iface1::smeth1()

The code compiles and executes fine. Will the spec be changed or will the codegen be changed to allow it to ILVerify?

**Steps to Reproduce**:
1.  Compile program
2. Run ilverify against program

** C# code**
using System;

namespace tr161758
{
    public interface iface1
    {
        static abstract void smeth1();
    }
    public class class1 : iface1
    {
        public static void smeth1()
        {
            Console.WriteLine("smeth1");

        }
    }
    public static class testclass
    {
        public static void testit<T>(T parm1) where T: iface1
        {
            T.smeth1();
        }
    }
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            class1 cvar = new class1();
            testclass.testit(cvar);
        }

    }
}
